      "id": "a1-u02-d01-t01",
      "before": "a1-u02-d01-e01",
      "title": { "en": "Być: to be" },
      "body": { "en": "A Polish verb changes its ending for each person. Here is *być*, \"to be\": the most used verb in Polish, and an irregular one. Today you learn *jestem*, \"I am\", and *jesteś*, \"you are\"." },
      "conjugation": {
        "verb": "być",
        "meaning": { "en": "to be" },
        "forms": { "ja": "jestem", "ty": "jesteś", "on": "jest", "my": "jesteśmy", "wy": "jesteście", "oni": "są" }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "a1-u02-d01-t02",
      "before": "a1-u02-d01-e06",
      "title": { "en": "You can leave out ja and ty" },
      "body": { "en": "The ending of the verb already says who is meant, so Poles usually say just *jestem Adam*. *Ja* and *ty* are added to contrast or to stress: *ja jestem Ewa, a ty?* Both ways are accepted here." }
    }
  ],
